Well, so much for Valkyries being the easiest. After sending hundreds of
the poor warrior women to their untimely demises in the dungeons of doom, I
decided that a different tactic was called for. Thus was born the long line
of Indy the Archaeologists. Indy (creative, yes?) IX achieved ascension
with aplomb.

Now, it is relevant to note that I did not increment the character version
unless the previous one lived at least until DL5 or so (which usually meant
minetown)...so while the suffix says 9, he was really Arc #49 or so after I
stopped with Valks.

It was a journey of great highs and great lows, but it ended up as high as
you can get, so there we are. The game started off innocently enough, on my
usual rounds of gathering funds and pet size for a protection run. Starting
with a pickaxe helps a lot for this, and I pretty well cored out levels 1-4
to gather up the coin and gems for the trip to the mines. Fortune smiled
with a co-aligned temple, and decent-if-not-spectacular shops. Hauling down
a chest from an earlier level, he proceeded to set up shop in the temple. A
quick 2400 gold later, and my AC was sitting pretty. A hobbit had helpfully
coughed up a +3 elven chain shirt, and a dwarf had "loaned" me his +2
cloak. The mines in this game were exceptionally long. Minetown was 3
levels down, and Mine's End was 4 levels below that. Did I haul every last
thing that dropped up to the temple? Why yes, yes I did. That took a while,
lemme tell ya. A 12 strength does not make for much inventory.

At mines end -1, I stumbled on (in the good way) a Polymorph Trap...an hour
of hauling my dog around by his leash netted a Gray Dragon pet. Tragedy
ensued when I brought him back up to the temple, and he proceeded to eat
the priest before I could leash him out. The silver lining is that the
priest was carrying a spellbook of identify, and *wow* did that help a lot.

Unfortunately, Dragons are not picky eaters, and my pet decided it would be
great fun (and this is after eating 3 wraith corpses out from under me!),
he decided to snack on a chameleon....and turned into a barbed devil. Not
as bad as say, a grid bug, but a serious downer nonetheless.

Sokoban fell easily and quickly, yielding my first BoH (oh, it gets good).
A stroke of fortune had me already carrying an amulet of reflection from a
soldier, so this was great serendipity. Heading down was fairly quick after
that, hovering at around level 13 or so, when a wraith gave his all to get
me up to doing the Quest. This is the furthest any of my characters have
ever been, so I was more than a little bit nervous. The Minion was a giant
pushover though. Orb in Hand (that's a reaaaaally nice artifact considering
it works from inventory...), I proceeded down. I moved my stash down to the
Oracle level when I got there, after buying candles from Izchak and socking
them away in my BoH, and was feeling cheery when I also found the Ludios
portal. Nothing very spectacular for drops, but Croesus's throne was in a
VERY giving mood, yielding up 2 wishes (SDSM, speed boots) before
vanishing.

In fact, this was a good throne game all around. Another throne on about
DL15 yielded Grayswandir, which replaced the Silver Saber I had been using
from the minetown captain after the Gray Dragon had finished introducing
him to his digestive tract. A nice change from the crap weapons that
Queztalquotal had been fobbing off on me in exchange for gratuitous blood
sacrifices.

The Big Room dropped a bones file...mostly junk, but a spare BoH (which
would become important), and a few other trinkets are always nice to have.
Magic whistle too, but I didn't get to enjoy it long because;

DL17 proved a great joy with a co-aligned temple. I immediately relocated
my stash there, but in the process, my Barbed Devil went un-tame, and I was
rendered petless. I think he's still down there....

Medusa and the Castle were anti-climatic...all I ever read about is how
hard the moat monsters are, but I just clobbered 'em. Never in any danger
at all. The biggest part about the Castle, was that I *FINALLY* got my
first scroll of enchant armor. I had at least a dozen weapon enchants (and
nothing to use them on)...I was starting to consider burning a wish for it
when it dropped, along with a wand of death from some of the barracks
soldiers.

Gehennom was actually more fun than I thought it would be, however I came
just *this* close to YASD from Juiblex. He engulfed me right on schedule,
and I /dig'd out of him right away and then gave him the kiss of death, but
I neglected to note the significance of the message that he had sickened
me. A couple of moves later, and my amulet of lifesaving gets put to the
test. I had long since had a uni horn of course, so I *could* have fixed it
if I had thought to, it just didn't occur to me. Oops. However, the
dungeons taketh, the dungeons giveth away. Right below Juiblex, I found
another one laying on the ground. This was a refreshing change from all of
the ones I had inadvertantly destroyed while monster bashing. I think in
the whole game, I ashed about a dozen "LS that were being worn by someone
else. As you can see below, I did end up with a slight surplus, however.

The entire quest almost came to an abrupt halt right after Baalzebub. He
was an annoying jerk, and escaped up 8 flights of stairs before he summoned
Geryon and I finally parked ME on the stairs and beat them both down. Well
and good, but then I decided to take my overflowing inventory up to my
stash and streamline a bit. Well, I had just previously run into a master
mind flayer, who had brain sucked me a little, and one of the consequences
of this was to make me forget what kind of wand was cancellation...ebony
wand went inside BoH, large quantity of fire and small fragments of cloth
came out. Highlights of the victims of this tragedy: Mojo (which had
dropped in Gehennom), my castle wand of wishing, with 2 charges on it, a
half dozen blessed full healing potions, my only ring of conflict, an
amulet of magical breathing, boots of water walking, a genocide scroll, a
bunch of gemstones, and oh yea, my 7 candles.

Yes, that's a problem. Izchak only had 3 more. So....hours, and hours, and
hours were spent scouring every single level of every single wing of the
dungeon collecting every tool I could get my hands on, followed by a prayer
to the gods and numerous polypile attempts. Using all of the charges on the
wand, I managed to get exactly 7 candles. I was, to say the least, a tidbit
relieved. Thankfully I had that bones piles BoH to haul all of that crap
around. 150-ish mirrors/pick axes/skeleton keys/lock picks/etc weigh a
*lot*.

Orcus died very satisfactorly to a rubber chicken when I came up the stairs
(I was going to do it more...legit...but then I hit a trapdoor and the
decision was taken from me), leaving behind not one, but TWO wands of
death.

Gehennom took me down to level 48...so it was a long slog. Vlad got laid
out by silver sabers, and Rodney got the business end of a death ray, and
then I was off to the races. By my count, I got bounced off the stairs 18
times 25% my lilly while patoot.

Now, maybe my level had something to do with it (without doing any wraith
luring from the Valley, I had ended up eating a lot of them, and not a few
potions of gain level), but the planes at level 25 were laughably easy
(well, helped for water to have levitation and water breathing, so I
couldn't be dragged under by the krakens/eels). Until the astral.

Wow...that place sucks. Upon entering, I was greeted by 2 Master Liches who
immediately started cursing all of my gear. I responded by blessedly
Genociding Ls. I had to that point avoided Genoing anything, but I was just
very tired of their little routine. Naturally, it was Altar #3 that was the
correct one, and it had Death in front of it. Since Rodney barely ever
showed up at all, I had tons of surplus beams'o'doom stored up, and they
aren't too useful for him. Fortunately, he seemed to get an attack of the
stupids after I killed him a couple times, because he decided to go
sightseeing in the corridor behind the temple and stopped bothering me
while I waded through the angelic hordes to reach my just reward.

